title: 'eth-rpc: keep the sync head behind un-indexed subscription gaps'
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    The subscription gap filler no longer lets the `sync-head` label advance over blocks it failed to index. Previously a gap fill that errored, or a gap dropped because the gap queue was full, still counted as done, so the head moved past the missing blocks and a restart never filled them. `eth_getLogs` then omitted those blocks' logs, and the lookups served from the index returned `null` or `0` for them: `eth_getTransactionReceipt`, `eth_getTransactionByHash`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockHashAndIndex`, `eth_getTransactionByBlockNumberAndIndex`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByHash`, `eth_getBlockTransactionCountByNumber`, and every method addressed by such a block's Ethereum block hash, whose mapping to the substrate block was never written. Methods that read the node, such as `eth_getBlockReceipts` by number and `eth_getTransactionCount`, were not affected. A finalized block whose own processing failed was skipped the same way; it is now handed to the gap filler like a missed block. A block the gap filler indexes does not evict a retracted sibling at the same height that was indexed while it was the best block; both stay in the index until a resync.

    A failed gap fill is now attempted up to 5 times, waiting 2, 4, 8 and then 16 seconds between attempts, about 30 seconds in all; an outage that outlasts them leaves the gap to the restart described below. In archive mode (`--eth-pruning=archive`) a gap that still fails, or that did not fit in the queue, keeps the sync head at its current block for the rest of the process lifetime. Later blocks are still indexed and served, but the index-backed methods listed above stay incomplete for the gap's blocks until the next restart, whose backward sync walks from the frozen head up to the current finalized block and extracts the blocks that are not yet indexed. Operators who see the error "in archive mode the sync head stays at its current block until a restart fills the gap" should restart eth-rpc. A restart fills the gap only once its cause has cleared. If the log shows `Sync stopped due to` after the restart, the gap could not be extracted and the sync head stays at its block. When the connected node no longer has the gap's state, point eth-rpc at an archive node and restart again; deleting the database and resyncing only helps with such a node. When the same block fails on every restart against an archive node, the failure is in eth-rpc itself: upgrade it and report the block. With `--eth-pruning <N>` there is no sync head and the index is in memory, so a restart does not help: the gap's blocks stay unindexed until they leave the retained window. Handing a finalized block to the gap filler because its processing failed, or because the first block of a stream could not be resolved, applies to archive mode only; with `--eth-pruning <N>` such a block is logged and left unindexed, as before.

    The fix prevents new holes only. An archive database that already lost a gap under the previous behaviour has the hole below its sync head, and nothing revisits it; delete the database and resync to repair it. No configuration changes are needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
